MKDIR(1)                       P32W Unix                        MKDIR(1)

NAME
       mkdir - make directories

SYNOPSIS
       mkdir [-p] [-m mode] dir ...

DESCRIPTION
       mkdir creates each directory in turn, with its . and ..
       entries, mode 0777 less the process's umask, owned by the
       effective user. The parent must exist and be writable. An
       error with one directory is reported (an existing one gives
       "mkdir: dir: file exists") and mkdir goes on with the next.

       A last component longer than 28 characters is refused (file
       name too long) rather than cut short.

OPTIONS
       -p     make the missing parents too, in order; an existing
              directory, on the way or at the end, is no error.
       -m mode
              give the new directory the octal mode (not less the
              umask), as chmod(1) would; parents made by -p get the
              usual mode.
       --     ends the options.

EXAMPLES
       mkdir src
       mkdir -p /mnt/backup/old
       mkdir -m 700 private

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every directory was made; 1 if one could not be (mkdir:
       dir: and the reason); 2 for a usage error (no directory, an
       unknown option, a mode that is not octal).

SEE ALSO
       rmdir(1), rm(1), mkdir(2), chmod(1)

BUGS
       -m takes only octal modes. sh(1) has no umask command.
